The flood insurance scenario is finished and delivered in `env/env_0065_flood_insurance_carrier/`. All three delivery conditions hold. Every difficulty passes its policy audit with the charter clean. Every rendered product passes its KB audit with no open KB or source items. The whole-scenario load check (`validate_scenario`) returns VALID, with 5 difficulties, 10 tasks, 69 tools and 353 documents.

The company is Tidemark Flood Insurance, which services federal (NFIP) flood policies and writes its own private flood products.

| Difficulty | Product | Policy audit rounds | KB audit |
|---|---|---|---|
| nfip_dwelling_servicing | federal home policies | 10 | PASS, round 2 |
| nfip_commercial_rcbap_claims | federal business and condo association policies | 12 | PASS, round 2 |
| private_homeflood_rating_billing | private home flood | 7 | PASS, round 4 |
| excess_flood_layering | excess layer above a primary policy | 10 | PASS, round 4 |
| lender_placed_flood_programs | lender-placed coverage | 12 | PASS, round 2 |

At merge, the blueprint was byte-identical to when it was frozen, and no business identifier repeated across difficulties. The two products no difficulty claims (private commercial flood and sensor-triggered parametric) passed their render checks only. As the process accepts, they were never audited document-by-document against their source.

**What it took:**
- **Federal realism:** the federal home product needed the most rework. Its first designs used made-up company "servicing allowances", which a federal-program carrier can't apply because FEMA sets the premiums and refunds. It passed once the tasks were rebuilt on real federal rules: waiting periods and their legal exceptions, the annual renewal cap, the federal surcharges and fees, and refund rules by cancellation reason.
- **Calculation-language limits:** the calculation language can't pick one record out of several of the same kind, which caused most of the extra rounds. Two of my own workarounds were rightly rejected by the auditors:
  - defining the multi-record case in words without computing it;
  - switching the pricing method based on how many records happened to exist.
  
  What passed was keying each lookup on the record tied to the event, such as the same date of loss or the address on the schedule. Where even that couldn't decide, the rule now books zero and says so.
- **Final cross-check:** with every product published, a last cross-product check found seeded records from other products that their own rules couldn't produce. These included out-of-range premiums and missing signed requests, ledgers and claim files. I fixed those in the task data only, with the rule and tool files byte-identical, so nothing had to be re-rendered.

**Known issues accepted:** a few defects sit in the frozen base product text and change no scored answer, so they were logged in the worklog rather than fixed:
- parameter descriptions that state rules an extended policy later overrides;
- small wording gaps in the two unclaimed products.

The worklog ends with a DELIVERED line. I also saved this run's lessons to memory: realism rules for federal-program products, and which workarounds auditors accept or reject.
